    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The ability to navigate one’s environment is a key aspect of individual survival and overall fitness in many species. For example, spatial navigation is important for foraging, finding potential mates, or returning home. A critical part of spatial navigation is exploration behavior, or how an individual behaves in a novel environment. Exploration is also an important step in building a cognitive map of a new environment. Interestingly, the propensity to explore a new environment can differ between males and females in many species. In mammals, females often have decreased exploratory behavior compared to males and some have suggested this difference is linked to reproductive strategy where females must stay close to home to care for offspring. However, little work has been performed on exploratory behavior in animals with contrasting reproductive strategies. To better understand how sex differences in exploratory behavior is linked to reproductive strategies, I quantified exploratory behavior in an open field assay with several species of South American poison frogs (Family Dendrobatidae) that vary in parental care strategies, including male- and female-uniparental and biparental care. I found no significant sex differences in exploration rate or average speed between sexes for any of the species, though there were significant differences among species, with the male uniparental species exhibiting higher exploratory behaviors than female uniparental and biparental species. Overall, this work shows exploratory behavior in dendrobatid poison frogs differs across species, but not sex, which may be linked to general life-history differences rather than parental care strategies.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
